{"name":"Golden Week","hex":"#ecd251","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Golden Week","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Any of several extended holidays celebrated in China and Japan, usually early in May.","A period of early voting time during Ohio's election season in which citizens can register to vote and cast an absentee ballot on the same day."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Golden_Week"}],"entries":[{"title":"Golden Week (China)","blocks":[{"text":"The Golden Week, in China, is the name given to three separate seven-day or eight-day national holidays, which were implemented in 2000:"},{"items":["Spring Festival, the Golden Week around the Chinese new year, begins in January or February.","The \"Labor Day Golden Week\" begins May 1 and was reintroduced in 2019 after being discontinued in 2007.","The \"National Day Golden Week\" begins around October 1. If Mid-Autumn Festival is near National Day, the Golden Week may be eight days long."]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/%E5%8D%81%E4%B8%80%E9%BB%84%E9%87%91%E5%91%A8%E7%9A%84%E4%BA%9A%E9%BE%99%E6%B9%BE.jpg/330px-%E5%8D%81%E4%B8%80%E9%BB%84%E9%87%91%E5%91%A8%E7%9A%84%E4%BA%9A%E9%BE%99%E6%B9%BE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_Week_(China)","wikidata":"Q1535381"},{"title":"Golden Week (Japan)","blocks":[{"text":"Golden Week  or Ōgon Shūkan (黄金週間) is a holiday period in Japan from 29 April to 5 May containing multiple public holidays. It is also known as Haru no Ōgata Renkyū ."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_Week_(Japan)","wikidata":"Q1137351"},{"title":"Week","blocks":[{"text":"A week is a unit of time equal to seven days. It is the standard calendrical period between a day and a month in most parts of the world. There are just over 52 weeks in a year, or on average 4+1⁄3 weeks in a month. The days of the week are often used to set work days, rest days, and holy days. The term \"week\" is also used to refer to a sub-section of the week, such as the workweek and weekend. Certain weeks within a year may be designated for a particular purpose, such as Golden Week in China and Japan. More informally, certain groups may advocate awareness weeks, such as National Family Week in Canada, which are designed to draw attention to a certain subject or cause."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/First_Day_of_Week_World_Map.svg/330px-First_Day_of_Week_World_Map.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Week","wikidata":"Q23387"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Italian_-_Bracelet_-_Walters_41269.jpg/500px-Italian_-_Bracelet_-_Walters_41269.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Italian_-_Bracelet_-_Walters_41269.jpg/1280px-Italian_-_Bracelet_-_Walters_41269.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Italian_-_Bracelet_-_Walters_41269.jpg/500px-Italian_-_Bracelet_-_Walters_41269.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An Italian cameo bracelet representing the days of the week by their eponymous deities (mid-19th century, Walters Art Museum)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AItalian_-_Bracelet_-_Walters_41269.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/CLM_14456_71r_detail.jpg/500px-CLM_14456_71r_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/CLM_14456_71r_detail.jpg/1280px-CLM_14456_71r_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/CLM_14456_71r_detail.jpg/500px-CLM_14456_71r_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Circular diagrams showing the division of the day and of the week, from a Carolingian ms. (Clm 14456 fol. 71r) of St. Emmeram Abbey. The week is divided into seven days, and each day into 24 hours, 96 puncta (quarter-hours), 240 minuta (tenths of an hour) and 960 momenta (40th parts of an hour).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACLM_14456_71r_detail.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Soviet_calendar_1930_color.jpg/500px-Soviet_calendar_1930_color.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Soviet_calendar_1930_color.jpg/1280px-Soviet_calendar_1930_color.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Soviet_calendar_1930_color.jpg/500px-Soviet_calendar_1930_color.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASoviet_calendar_1930_color.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Soviet_calendar_1933_color.jpg/500px-Soviet_calendar_1933_color.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Soviet_calendar_1933_color.jpg/1280px-Soviet_calendar_1933_color.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&ut